{"number":12,"title":"Fractions of a Collection","slug":"fractions-collection","color":"#db2777","level":"","estTime":"","description":"Learn Fractions of a Collection with interactive diagrams, worked examples, and practice tests for Grade 2 students.","topics":[{"title":"Fractions of a Collection","concept":"<p class=\"eb-p\">Welcome to the world of fractions! So far, we have looked at whole numbers (like <em class=\"math\">1, 2,</em> and <em class=\"math\">5</em>). But sometimes, we need to talk about numbers that are smaller than a single whole thing. A <strong>fraction</strong> represents a small slice or part of a whole item.</p><ul class=\"concept-list\"><li><strong>Whole vs. Part Concept:</strong> * A <strong>Whole</strong> is an entire object, shape, or collection that is completely unbroken and uncut (like a whole round pizza or an entire candy bar).</li><li>A <strong>Part</strong> is a smaller piece or section that has been sliced or separated from the whole.</li></ul><ul class=\"concept-list\"><li><strong>Equal vs. Unequal Parts:</strong> Fractions <em>only</em> exist when a whole is cut into <strong>equal parts</strong>.</li><li><strong>Equal Parts:</strong> Slices that are exactly the same size and shape.</li><li><strong>Unequal Parts:</strong> Slices that are different sizes. If a sibling gets a giant piece of cake and you get a tiny crumb, that is unfair because the parts are unequal! We cannot use mathematical fractions for unequal parts.</li></ul><ul class=\"concept-list\"><li><strong>Introduction to Core Fractions:</strong> When you divide a whole into equal pieces, those pieces get special math names:</li><li><strong>Half (<em class=\"math\">\\frac{1}{2}</em>):</strong> Created when a whole is divided into exactly <strong>2 equal parts</strong>. One of those pieces is one-half.</li><li><strong>One-Third (<em class=\"math\">\\frac{1}{3}</em>):</strong> Created when a whole is divided into exactly <strong>3 equal parts</strong>. One of those pieces is one-third.</li><li><strong>One-Fourth or Quarter (<em class=\"math\">\\frac{1}{4}</em>):</strong> Created when a whole is divided into exactly <strong>4 equal parts</strong>. One of those pieces is a quarter.</li></ul><ul class=\"concept-list\"><li><strong>Writing Simple Unit Fractions:</strong> A fraction is written using two numbers stacked vertically with a flat line in the middle:</li><li>The <strong>Bottom Number</strong> (Denominator) tells you the <em>total number of equal pieces</em> the whole was cut into.</li><li>The <strong>Top Number</strong> (Numerator) tells you <em>how many pieces</em> you are looking at, selecting, or eating.</li><li>A <strong>Unit Fraction</strong> always has a <em class=\"math\">1</em> on top because it talks about just <em>one</em> single piece of the whole (like <em class=\"math\">\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{3},</em> or <em class=\"math\">\\frac{1}{4}</em>).</li></ul><ul class=\"concept-list\"><li><strong>Shading Regional Fractions:</strong> Coloring in a specific number of equal sections inside a shape to match a fraction statement visually.</li></ul>","visual":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 378\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:600px;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto;border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\"><defs><linearGradient id=\"bg\" x1=\"0%\" y1=\"0%\" x2=\"100%\" y2=\"100%\"><stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#fdf2f8\"/><stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#fce7f3\"/></linearGradient></defs><rect width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" fill=\"url(#bg)\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"600\" height=\"46\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#db2777\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text stroke=\"none\" x=\"300\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-family=\"system-ui,sans-serif\">Fractions of a Collection — Fractions of Sets</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"58\" width=\"560\" height=\"120\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fce7f3\" stroke=\"#db2777\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text stroke=\"none\" x=\"300\" y=\"78\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#9d174d\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-family=\"system-ui,sans-serif\">Fractions of a Group/Collection</text><text stroke=\"none\" x=\"300\" y=\"98\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#374151\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" font-family=\"system-ui,sans-serif\">12 stars total. Circle shows 1/4.</text><polygon points=\"50.0,126.0 54.11449676604731,134.33688103937536 63.31479122813215,135.67376207875074 56.657395614066075,142.16311896062464 58.22899353209462,151.32623792124926 50.0,147.0 41.77100646790538,151.32623792124926 43.342604385933925,142.16311896062464 36.68520877186785,135.67376207875074 45.88550323395269,134.33688103937536\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" stroke=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"94.0,126.0 98.11449676604731,134.33688103937536 107.31479122813215,135.67376207875074 100.65739561406608,142.16311896062464 102.22899353209462,151.32623792124926 94.0,147.0 85.77100646790538,151.32623792124926 87.34260438593392,142.16311896062464 80.68520877186785,135.67376207875074 89.88550323395269,134.33688103937536\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" stroke=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"138.0,126.0 142.11449676604732,134.33688103937536 151.31479122813215,135.67376207875074 144.65739561406608,142.16311896062464 146.22899353209462,151.32623792124926 138.0,147.0 129.77100646790538,151.32623792124926 131.34260438593392,142.16311896062464 124.68520877186785,135.67376207875074 133.88550323395268,134.33688103937536\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" stroke=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"182.0,126.0 186.11449676604732,134.33688103937536 195.31479122813215,135.67376207875074 188.65739561406608,142.16311896062464 190.22899353209462,151.32623792124926 182.0,147.0 173.77100646790538,151.32623792124926 175.34260438593392,142.16311896062464 168.68520877186785,135.67376207875074 177.88550323395268,134.33688103937536\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" stroke=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"226.0,126.0 230.11449676604732,134.33688103937536 239.31479122813215,135.67376207875074 232.65739561406608,142.16311896062464 234.22899353209462,151.32623792124926 226.0,147.0 217.77100646790538,151.32623792124926 219.34260438593392,142.16311896062464 212.68520877186785,135.67376207875074 221.88550323395268,134.33688103937536\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" stroke=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"270.0,126.0 274.1144967660473,134.33688103937536 283.31479122813215,135.67376207875074 276.65739561406605,142.16311896062464 278.22899353209465,151.32623792124926 270.0,147.0 261.77100646790535,151.32623792124926 263.34260438593395,142.16311896062464 256.68520877186785,135.67376207875074 265.8855032339527,134.33688103937536\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" stroke=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"314.0,126.0 318.1144967660473,134.33688103937536 327.31479122813215,135.67376207875074 320.65739561406605,142.16311896062464 322.22899353209465,151.32623792124926 314.0,147.0 305.77100646790535,151.32623792124926 307.34260438593395,142.16311896062464 300.68520877186785,135.67376207875074 309.8855032339527,134.33688103937536\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" stroke=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"358.0,126.0 362.1144967660473,134.33688103937536 371.31479122813215,135.67376207875074 364.65739561406605,142.16311896062464 366.22899353209465,151.32623792124926 358.0,147.0 349.77100646790535,151.32623792124926 351.34260438593395,142.16311896062464 344.68520877186785,135.67376207875074 353.8855032339527,134.33688103937536\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" stroke=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"402.0,126.0 406.1144967660473,134.33688103937536 415.31479122813215,135.67376207875074 408.65739561406605,142.16311896062464 410.22899353209465,151.32623792124926 402.0,147.0 393.77100646790535,151.32623792124926 395.34260438593395,142.16311896062464 388.68520877186785,135.67376207875074 397.8855032339527,134.33688103937536\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" stroke=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"446.0,126.0 450.1144967660473,134.33688103937536 459.31479122813215,135.67376207875074 452.65739561406605,142.16311896062464 454.22899353209465,151.32623792124926 446.0,147.0 437.77100646790535,151.32623792124926 439.34260438593395,142.16311896062464 432.68520877186785,135.67376207875074 441.8855032339527,134.33688103937536\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" stroke=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"490.0,126.0 494.1144967660473,134.33688103937536 503.31479122813215,135.67376207875074 496.65739561406605,142.16311896062464 498.22899353209465,151.32623792124926 490.0,147.0 481.77100646790535,151.32623792124926 483.34260438593395,142.16311896062464 476.68520877186785,135.67376207875074 485.8855032339527,134.33688103937536\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" stroke=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"534.0,126.0 538.1144967660473,134.33688103937536 547.3147912281321,135.67376207875074 540.6573956140661,142.16311896062464 542.2289935320946,151.32623792124926 534.0,147.0 525.7710064679054,151.32623792124926 527.3426043859339,142.16311896062464 520.6852087718678,135.67376207875074 529.8855032339527,134.33688103937536\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" stroke=\"none\"/><rect x=\"22\" y=\"115\" width=\"180\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#db2777\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text stroke=\"none\" x=\"300\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#9d174d\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-family=\"system-ui,sans-serif\">3 out of 12 are gold = 3/12 = 1/4</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"190\" width=\"560\" height=\"90\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#e2e8f0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text stroke=\"none\" x=\"300\" y=\"210\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#1e293b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-family=\"system-ui,sans-serif\">Comparing Fractions with Same Denominator</text><text stroke=\"none\" x=\"300\" y=\"235\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#374151\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" font-family=\"system-ui,sans-serif\">3/8 &lt; 5/8     (more pieces = bigger fraction when denominator same)</text><text stroke=\"none\" x=\"300\" y=\"258\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#64748b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" font-family=\"system-ui,sans-serif\">Larger numerator = larger fraction when bottom number is the same!</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"292\" width=\"560\" height=\"75\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text stroke=\"none\" x=\"300\" y=\"312\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#15803d\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-family=\"system-ui,sans-serif\">Equivalent Fractions</text><text stroke=\"none\" x=\"150\" y=\"335\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#15803d\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-family=\"system-ui,sans-serif\">1/2</text><text stroke=\"none\" x=\"300\" y=\"335\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#374151\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-family=\"system-ui,sans-serif\">=</text><text stroke=\"none\" x=\"450\" y=\"335\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#15803d\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-family=\"system-ui,sans-serif\">2/4</text><text stroke=\"none\" x=\"300\" y=\"355\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#4b5563\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" font-family=\"system-ui,sans-serif\">Same amount, different number of pieces!</text></svg>","examples":[{"number":1,"problem":"<p class=\"eb-p\">A standard circle is divided into <em class=\"math\">3</em> completely equal, matching wedges. One of the wedges is colored bright blue. Write down the mathematical fraction that represents the blue part of the circle.</p>","solution":"<ol class=\"solution-steps\"><li>Count the total number of equal sections the whole circle is cut into: There are <em class=\"math\">3</em> equal sections. This value goes on the <strong>bottom</strong> of the fraction line.</li><li>Count how many sections are colored blue: Only <em class=\"math\">1</em> section is blue. This value goes on the <strong>top</strong> of the fraction line.</li><li>Stack the two values together with a fraction bar:</li></ol><div class=\"solution-answer\">Answer: <em class=\"math\">\\frac{1}{3}</em> (One-third)</div>"}],"recap":["A <strong>fraction</strong> shows the relationship between a single smaller part and a complete unbroken whole.","You can only write a fraction if the shapes or items are cut into <strong>perfectly equal sizes</strong>.","The <strong>denominator</strong> (bottom number) is the total count of equal slices. The <strong>numerator</strong> (top number) is the count of parts you are talking about.","<strong>Unit fractions</strong> always feature a 1 as their top digit (numerator)."],"quiz":[{"q":"A fraction can describe part of a ___ of objects.","options":["shape","group","line","number"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A group/collection."},{"q":"Half of $10$ is:","options":["$2$","$5$","$10$","$20$"],"correct":1,"explanation":"$5$."},{"q":"One fourth of $8$ is:","options":["$2$","$4$","$8$","$1$"],"correct":0,"explanation":"$2$."},{"q":"To find half of a group, share it into ___ equal parts.","options":["$2$","$3$","$4$","$10$"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Two parts."}]}],"tests":{"online":{"title":"Fractions of a Collection — Practice Test","minutes":15,"questions":[{"q":"10 marbles 1/2 are blue. How many blue?","options":["2","4","5","6"],"correct":2,"explanation":"1/2 of 10 = 5","difficulty":"easy"},{"q":"1/3 of 12 apples = ?","options":["3","4","6","8"],"correct":1,"explanation":"12/3 = 4","difficulty":"easy"},{"q":"8 stars 3/4 are yellow. How many yellow?","options":["2","4","6","8"],"correct":2,"explanation":"1/4 of 8=2; 3/4=6","difficulty":"medium"},{"q":"20 students 1/4 absent. How many absent?","options":["4","5","10","15"],"correct":1,"explanation":"1/4 of 20 = 5","difficulty":"medium"},{"q":"1/2 of 18 = ?","options":["6","7","9","10"],"correct":2,"explanation":"18/2 = 9","difficulty":"easy"},{"q":"15 flowers 1/3 are red. How many red?","options":["3","5","6","8"],"correct":1,"explanation":"15/3 = 5","difficulty":"medium"},{"q":"Which collection shows 1/2?","options":["3 of 8","4 of 8","3 of 6","5 of 8"],"correct":1,"explanation":"4/8 = 1/2","difficulty":"medium"},{"q":"1/4 of 16 = ?","options":["2","4","6","8"],"correct":1,"explanation":"16/4 = 4","difficulty":"easy"},{"q":"24 books 1/3 are comics. How many comics?","options":["6","8","12","16"],"correct":1,"explanation":"24/3 = 8","difficulty":"medium"},{"q":"6 of 12 balloons are red. Fraction red?","options":["1/3","1/2","2/3","3/4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"6/12 = 1/2","difficulty":"medium"}]},"imo":{"title":"Fractions of a Collection — Olympiad Practice","minutes":10,"questions":[{"q":"3/4 of 16 marbles are blue. How many blue?","options":["4","8","12","16"],"correct":2,"explanation":"1/4 of 16=4; 3/4=12","difficulty":"medium"},{"q":"20 birds. 2/5 are parrots. How many parrots?","options":["4","6","8","10"],"correct":2,"explanation":"1/5 of 20=4; 2/5=8","difficulty":"hard"},{"q":"30 students 1/3 wear glasses. How many do NOT?","options":["10","15","20","25"],"correct":2,"explanation":"1/3=10 wear glasses; 30-10=20 do not","difficulty":"medium"},{"q":"6 of 18 apples are green. Fraction NOT green?","options":["1/3","2/3","1/6","5/6"],"correct":1,"explanation":"6/18=1/3 green; 2/3 not green","difficulty":"hard"},{"q":"Team has 12 members. 1/4 are girls. How many boys?","options":["3","6","8","9"],"correct":3,"explanation":"1/4 of 12=3 girls; 12-3=9 boys","difficulty":"medium"},{"q":"Meera has 24 crayons. Gives 1/3 to Sana and 1/4 to Tina. Left?","options":["8","10","12","14"],"correct":1,"explanation":"8+6=14 given away; 24-14=10","difficulty":"hard"}]}}}